# ComposeUX Reactive Embedded Tier [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

> Today an ESP32 fetches a config and renders it once, or emits a hardcoded message. This makes it *reactive*: an on-device bus client + the binding/behavior engine + the on-chip DSL, so a value on the bus drives a widget, an actuator, or a derived output — live. The reactive CORE is board-agnostic and runs on three ESP32 variants; two reference boards prove the abstraction is real.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-ER-INTENT] Make the MCU a Live Node

- definition: build the reactive engine in C++ for the MCU — an **on-device bus client** (MQTT now; CAN later, [CXC-PS-CAN]) + a **namespace store** + the **binding/behavior engine** (the Python `[SPEC:CXC-BIND]`/`[CXC-HEADLESS]` model ported) + the **on-chip DSL** (2b, `cyd/src/dsl.{h,cpp}`, already proven on ESP32). A bus value now *drives* something on-device instead of being ignored.
- it is a PORT against a working reference (the Python engine + the desktop goldens), the same shape 2b was — the hardest de-risk (DSL float64 on ESP32) is already retired.
- foundation: `firmware/esp32_mqtt_client/` (an ESP32-C3 MQTT ComposeBus client — WiFi + PubSubClient) + the portable DSL — R1 wires these together.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

---

## 2. Architecture

### [CXC-ER-CORE] The Board-Agnostic Reactive Core

- definition: one C++ core, independent of display/board: bus client → namespace store → on value-change, re-evaluate the bindings/behaviors that read that namespace (calling the DSL for `expr:`), then apply the result to an **output** — a widget attribute (display boards), a physical actuator (HAL), or a published derived namespace (headless logic). Headless-capable by construction (no render dependency), exactly like the Python `RenderApp(headless=True)`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-BOARDS] One Core, Three Variants (the abstraction shake-out)

- the core runs on three connected ESP32 variants, proving the board-profile seam ([CXC-RM-M5]) across genuinely different silicon:
  - **ESP32-C3** (RISC-V, bare / the actuator boards) — headless reactive node; re-verifies the DSL on RISC-V (2b was Xtensa-only).
  - **CYD ESP32** (Xtensa dual, SPI ILI9341) — reactive + render (the constrained banded-display floor).
  - **Qualia ESP32-S3** (RGB666 parallel, framebuffer) — reactive + render; unlocks live transforms.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-HAL] On-Device Input + Output HAL

- the HAL ([SPEC:CXC-HAL]) realized on the MCU: **inputs** (GPIO/ADC/I2C sensors) read → publish namespaces; **outputs** (GPIO/PWM/relay/driver actuators) subscribe → drive. Grounded by the real boards — lego_dots (12× GPIO light switch, Output Tier 1) and rack_triple_gauge (steppers via MCP23017/L293D, Output Tier 2). "Define once, actuate many" ([CXC-HAL-ACTUATE]): a `gauge/value` drives a screen needle AND a stepper needle.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-RENDER] Render Integration + Dirty-Band (display boards)

- on the CYD/Qualia, a bound value drives a widget attribute → **re-render only the changed region** (dirty-band). Essential on the banded ILI9341; on the Qualia framebuffer it also enables live transforms. Extends the M5 renderer, not a rewrite.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DEVICE] The Device Model (local bus + bridge policy)

- realizes [CXC-HAL-DEVICE]: the device has a **local bus**; HAL inputs/outputs, widgets, and logic connect to it; a **per-namespace bridge policy** decides what crosses to the shared ComposeBus (a reading can stay local/self-contained or bridge). A device config is a composition — render widgets + HAL descriptors + logic + bridge policy. Participation is first-class here ([CXC-HAL-TOPOLOGY]); the relayed CH32 tier is deferred.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

---

## 3. Slices (hardware-in-the-loop; dispatcher flashes + verifies)

### [CXC-ER-SLICES] R1–R5 (David-ratified 2026-07-10; CH32 deferred)

- **R1 — reactive core on the bare ESP32-C3 (headless)**: bus client + namespace store + binding/behavior eval calling the on-chip DSL; board-agnostic. Acceptance: clean `pio run -e esp32c3`; the DSL corpus re-run on the C3 (RISC-V re-verify); a Mac publish → the C3 evaluates → GPIO/serial.
- **R2 — output-actuator HAL on the C3 → bring up the two built boards**: lego_dots then rack_triple_gauge.
- **R3 — render integration on the CYD**: core + bind→widget-attr + dirty-band (extends M5).
- **R4 — core on the ESP32-S3** (Qualia, headless foundation).
- **R5 — Qualia RGB666 display** (parallel panel + framebuffer + live transforms).
- (deferred: the CH32V003 relayed daughtercard, button_matrix_4x4.)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

---

## 4. Compliance

| ID | Requirement | Testable Criterion |
|----|-------------|-------------------|
| CXC-ER-C1 | Reactive core | A bus value-change re-evaluates the bindings that read it and applies the result; headless (no render dependency) |
| CXC-ER-C2 | On-chip DSL | `expr:` bindings evaluate via `cyd/src/dsl`; the corpus re-runs correct on each variant (incl. RISC-V C3) |
| CXC-ER-C3 | Board-agnostic | The same core compiles + runs on C3 / CYD / S3; per-variant differences are board-profile only |
| CXC-ER-C4 | Output actuator | A subscribed namespace drives a GPIO/PWM/driver output (lego_dots, rack_triple_gauge) |
| CXC-ER-C5 | Render + dirty-band | On CYD/Qualia a bound value re-renders only the changed region |
| CXC-ER-C6 | Live transforms | On the Qualia framebuffer, rotation/scale render (the CYD cannot) |
| CXC-ER-C7 | Local bus + bridge | Per-namespace bridge policy: a reading stays local or bridges to the shared bus |
| CXC-ER-C8 | Two-board abstraction | The core runs unmodified on ≥2 reference boards; board differences isolated to the profile |
| CXC-ER-C9 | DRADIS bus contract | `dradis/contacts` (JSON array; `id`/`type`/`bearing`/`range`/`allegiance`, `range` in light-seconds) drives the scope; malformed JSON warns-once → empty, never hangs; own-ship is not a contact |
| CXC-ER-C10 | Log projection | A contact projects to `radius_px = clamp(220 + 61.86·log₁₀(range_ls), 0, R)` on the ×60 light-clock; the three rings render at R/3, 2R/3, R |
| CXC-ER-C11 | Consumer-derived heading | A contact that moves between updates shows a directional indicator pointing along its screen-space `atan2(dy,dx)` motion; a still contact shows none — heading is never read from the bus |
| CXC-ER-C12 | State-on-bus behaviors | Hostile contacts blink ~2 Hz while `demo/alert` holds (one bit on the bus, blink in the widget); the HUD tally is derived from the array, never published separately |
| CXC-ER-C13 | Stellar-bodies backdrop | `dradis/bodies` (JSON array; `id`/`kind`/`type`/`name`/`bearing`/`range_ls`/`size`) renders UNDER contacts through the same log projection, with NO threat blink, NO motion indicator, and excluded from the HUD tally |
| CXC-ER-C14 | Fog-of-war = zero display change | Republishing the same `id` with an evolved `type` (and `allegiance`) morphs the icon in place; the display adds NO identification/threshold logic — `type` is the sensed classification, not ground truth |
| CXC-ER-C15 | Remote restart honored | Publishing `_device/<id>/restart` OR broadcast `_device/restart` with a truthy payload restarts the device; it re-runs boot-fetch and loads the YAML PUT to bus_storage after its last boot — provable via boot-counter / boot-fetch log line / observed config GET ([CXC-ER-REMOTE-RESTART]) |
| CXC-ER-C16 | Restart ack closes the loop | The device publishes `_device/<id>/status` = "restarting" before `esp_restart()` and re-announces presence (`_bus/discovery/<id>`) after boot; a silent restart is disallowed ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS]) |
| CXC-ER-C17 | Config-reload, NOT firmware-OTA | A remote restart re-fetches the YAML but runs the SAME firmware; a new firmware capability (e.g. a newly-ported renderer) is NOT delivered and still needs a USB flash / a future ESP32-OTA capability ([CXC-ER-REMOTE-RESTART]) |
| CXC-ER-C18 | Truthy-only + boot-loop safe | A truthy payload (`dsl_truthy` canon) restarts; a falsy/empty payload is a NO-OP; the command is non-retained and restart commands within the boot-guard window are ignored, so a re-delivered/doubled command cannot loop the device ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS]) |
| CXC-ER-C19 | Shared path, all envs | The handler lives in the shared firmware app shell, not a board profile; cyd / qualia_display / esp32c3 and future variants inherit remote-restart unmodified ([CXC-ER-REMOTE-RESTART]) |

---

## 5. Capability: The DRADIS Contacts Widget

> A worked example of the reactive tier's governing seam — **state on the bus, behavior in the widget** — on a display widget. `dradis_contacts` renders a Battlestar-style radar scope from a bus-published contact array; the bus carries only *what is out there* (semantic position), the widget owns *how it looks and moves* (all derivation + temporal behavior). It is the canonical decentralization exemplar: every derivable quantity (heading, threat count, blink phase) is reconstructed at the leaf, never transmitted.

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS] The `dradis_contacts` Reactive Radar-Contact Widget

- definition: a headless-authored display widget for the reactive-render tier ([CXC-ER-RENDER], lands on the R5 Qualia surface): it subscribes to a contact array on the bus and re-renders the scope on change. The narrow waist is the bus contract ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-BUS]) — the `dradis_contacts` widget and the scenario driver (a bus peer that spawns/moves contacts) each lower to THAT, never to each other.
- see-also: There is a BIGGER, richer DRADIS — the full BSG jump/hunt tactical GAME — in its own repo at (a local path, not published) (registered as [PROJECT-BSG-GAME] in `decisions/projects.lts`; specs `bsg/spec/{10-dradis-game,11-dradis-physical-model,12-gm-command-seam}.lts`). That game models a whole universe (star map, planets, fleets, FTL, combat, fog-of-war) and PUBLISHES the `dradis/contacts` + `dradis/bodies` arrays THIS widget consumes. The relationship is deliberate and load-bearing: the game owns *what is out there* (the world sim + sensor); this widget is the platform-pure RENDER surface that owns *how it looks* — they meet only at the bus contract ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-BUS]), never at each other. If you need the sensor/scenario/universe side, it lives in the bsg repo, NOT here; this spec is the display leaf only.
- key-insight: this is [CXC-BIND-PRINCIPLE] ("state on the bus, behavior in the widget") realized end-to-end on a non-trivial widget: position/allegiance/alert-bit are semantic state on the bus; projection, motion heading, blink phase, and counts are all widget-owned. The demo's "reactive" is exactly the behavior the widget owns.
- own-ship: the Battlestar (own-ship) is NOT a contact — it is a static center `image` widget in the scene. Contact/threat counts are DERIVED by the widget from the array, never published separately ([DP-CIO-3] pushed to the display leaf).
- config: appears in `.compose.yaml` as `type: dradis_contacts` with `center_x`/`center_y`/`radius`, `hud: true`, a `source: {namespace: dradis/contacts, default: "[]"}` block, and an `alert: {namespace: demo/alert}` block.
- change: "REMOVED ping-on-sweep-pass (beam/contact coupling): when the radar sweep beam passed over a contact it fired a ping cue. Deleted entirely — the `beam:` binding and the sweep-angle read go with it. The `sweep` widget still renders as the visual beam; it no longer drives any contact behavior."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The sweep beam was coupled to contacts: crossing a contact triggered a per-contact ping cue, computed each frame from the live sweep angle vs each contact's bearing."
  - reasoning: "On this tier the coupling fired inconsistently (angular sampling vs frame cadence made the crossing detection miss or double-fire) and the per-frame sweep-angle read against every contact cost real horsepower on a banded/framebuffer display that otherwise repaints only on bus change. The cue was decorative; the cost was structural (it defeated the event-driven publish-on-change repaint model by needing continuous per-frame evaluation)."
  - rejected_alternative: "Fix the sampling (sub-frame angular interpolation + hysteresis on the crossing) — rejected: it buys back a decorative cue by reintroducing an always-on per-frame compute loop, the exact thing the reactive tier's dirty-band/publish-on-change design exists to avoid ([CXC-ER-RENDER])."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That beam/contact coupling is not worth a continuous per-frame cost on this tier. If a future tier (Qualia framebuffer with idle headroom, or a design where the sweep cue is load-bearing rather than decorative) makes the per-frame budget free, reopen it — the visual `sweep` widget is still present to re-couple against."
  - implication: "The `sweep` widget is now purely visual; no contact behavior depends on the beam. Contact cues are driven only by bus state (alert) and widget-derived motion, both of which fit the publish-on-change model."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-BUS] The Bus Contract — `dradis/contacts` + `demo/alert`

- definition: **`dradis/contacts`** is a JSON **array** of contact objects, published by any peer (scenario driver, an Intelligence, a sensor); the widget subscribes and re-renders on change. Fields per contact: `id` (stable string — the widget tracks a contact across updates by `id` so it can animate motion, not respawn); `type` (icon key → `bus://assets/icons/dradis/<type>.bmp`); `bearing` (degrees, **0 = up/12-o'clock, clockwise positive**); `range` (**physical distance in LIGHT-SECONDS**, float > 0); `allegiance` (`hostile`|`friendly`|`neutral` — drives tint + blink policy); `size` (optional px, default 72).
- alert-namespace: **`demo/alert`** — truthy ⇒ RED ALERT; hostile contacts blink while it holds ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-BLINK]). One bit; the temporal behavior is widget-owned.
- robustness: empty / `"[]"` source → no contacts, no crash; malformed JSON → warn-once + empty, never a hang.
- sensed-classification: `type` is the **currently SENSED classification, not necessarily ground truth** — the publisher owns the sensor model and may report `large_unknown`/`small_unknown` for an unresolved contact, republishing the **same `id`** so the icon morphs in place as it resolves ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-IDENT]; the sensor/identification model that owns identification + the threat-flip is the publishing application's sensor model, an app-layer concern). Full staged icon registry (all `bus://assets/icons/dradis/<type>.bmp`): `battlestar` (own-ship), `basestar`, `resurrection_ship`, `raider`, `raider_squadron`, `heavy_raider`, `viper`, `viper_squadron`, `raptor`, `civilian1`, `civilian2`, `civilian3`, `large_unknown`, `small_unknown`.
- change: "`range` semantics changed from a 0..1 normalized screen fraction to a PHYSICAL quantity — light-seconds (float > 0, typical 0.01…60) — rendered on a logarithmic scope ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-PROJ]); edge = 60 ls = 1 light-minute."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "`range` was a 0..1 fraction: 0 = scope center, 1 = scope edge. The publisher pre-normalized distance to a screen position; the widget multiplied by the radius linearly."
  - reasoning: "That put a display concern (where on the glass) into the contract and made the number meaningless off this one scope — two scopes of different range spans could not share a contact array, and the publisher had to know the display's radius to place a contact. Making `range` a physical light-second distance moves the projection (including the log compression) entirely into the widget: the bus now states a real-world fact ('this contact is 12 light-seconds out'), and any scope renders it correctly at its own scale. It also unlocks a logarithmic scope, which a 0..1 fraction structurally cannot express."
  - rejected_alternative: "Keep 0..1 and add a separate `range_ls` field — rejected: two fields for one quantity, with the display-coupled one still authoritative for placement; the publisher would still be pre-normalizing to a screen fraction, which is the thing being removed."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That a radar contact's distance is a physical quantity the display projects, not a screen fraction the publisher pre-computes. If a consumer genuinely needs the publisher to own placement (e.g. a non-metric abstract scope with no physical range), that consumer wants a different widget, not this field overloaded back to a fraction."
  - implication: "Publishers emit real distances and are decoupled from any scope's geometry; the widget owns the linear-in-log₁₀ projection and the ring calibration. The scenario driver closes hostiles from ~60 ls toward ~1 ls over the timeline."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-PROJ] The Logarithmic Light-Clock Projection

- definition: the scope is a **×60 light-clock** — total range 1 light-minute (60 ls), three concentric rings each a factor of 60, evenly spaced in radius. Radius is **linear in log₁₀(range_ls)**, anchored on the rings `(radius_px, range_ls) = (R/3, 1/60), (2R/3, 1), (R, 60)` (defaults R = 330 → 110/220/330).
- projection: `radius_px = clamp(220 + 61.86·log₁₀(range_ls), 0, R)` where `61.86 = 110 / log₁₀(60)`; `θ = bearing·π/180`; `x = center_x + radius_px·sin(θ)`; `y = center_y − radius_px·cos(θ)` (minus so bearing 0 points UP). Icon blitted centered at `(x, y)`, clipped to the scope circle.
- rings: three faint circles + labels — inner `1/60 LS` (~5,000 km) @ R/3, middle `1 LS` (~300,000 km) @ 2R/3, edge `1 LM` (60 ls) @ R. A contact nearer than 1/60 ls clamps toward center; beyond 60 ls clamps to the edge (or is dropped).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-MOTION] Directional Motion Indicator — the Decentralization Exemplar

- definition: every *moving* contact gets a directional pointer drawn BEHIND its sprite, rotated to point along its direction of motion. The heading is **NOT on the bus** — the widget DERIVES it locally: it already tracks each contact by stable `id`, keeps the previous projected `(x, y)`, and each update computes `heading = atan2(dy, dx)` from the **screen-space** delta (the visual motion the eye sees, already log-projected). Zero new contract fields.
- key-insight: this is the corpus's canonical exemplar of the decentralization principle — **complexity/derivation flows to the leaf; the bus carries only semantic position, never derivable mechanism.** The consumer already holds every input the heading needs (it holds the position stream by construction), so transmitting a heading would be transmitting a quantity the receiver can already reconstruct. The bus stays purely positional; the display reconstructs the motion.
- render-detail: the asset `directional_indicator.bmp` points DOWN by default (tip toward +y / 6-o'clock), so the applied rotation is `heading − (π/2)`. Render ONLY when motion exceeds a small epsilon (a still contact shows no vector); smooth the heading slightly so jitter doesn't spin it. Draw order per contact: indicator first, sprite on top.
- change: "Contact heading is DERIVED by the display widget from successive projected positions, NOT published on the bus. No `heading`/`course`/`velocity` field exists in the contract."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The natural first design adds a `heading` (or `course`) field to each contact so the widget can orient the motion indicator directly from the bus."
  - reasoning: "The consumer that draws the indicator already holds successive positions of the same contact (it tracks by stable `id` across updates, and it projects each position anyway). Heading is a pure function of those positions it already has — atan2 of the screen-space delta. Publishing it would put a derivable, redundant quantity on the bus, one that must be kept consistent with the positions it is derived from (a publisher could emit a heading that disagrees with the position delta). Deriving at the leaf makes inconsistency impossible and keeps the contract minimal and purely positional. Deriving in SCREEN space (post-log-projection) is also more correct for a motion indicator: it points along the visual motion the operator sees, which the log scope has already compressed — a bus-space heading would not match the on-glass vector."
  - rejected_alternative: "Publish a `heading` field computed by whoever moves the contact — rejected: redundant with position, a new consistency burden (heading-vs-position drift), and it would be a *bus-space* heading that disagrees with the log-projected on-screen motion the indicator actually depicts."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That the display holds the inputs to reconstruct the heading (the position stream, tracked by stable id). If a source ever emits a heading that is NOT derivable from the positions it publishes — e.g. a true instantaneous course from an inertial sensor that the sampled positions cannot recover — then that heading is genuine new information, not a derivable mechanism, and belongs on the bus as its own semantic field."
  - implication: "The contract carries no motion fields; any peer publishing only positions gets correct motion indicators for free. The same reasoning governs the derived HUD tally ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-HUD]) and the derived counts — the widget reconstructs, the bus does not transmit."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-BLINK] Blink on Alert — State on Bus, Behavior in Widget

- definition: `allegiance == "hostile"` AND `demo/alert` truthy ⇒ the contact blinks ~2 Hz. The alert state is ONE bit on the bus; the temporal blink (frame cadence, phase) lives entirely in the widget — animation frames are never streamed. This is [CXC-BIND-PRINCIPLE] (Problem 4) on the reactive tier: a peer flips one bit at intent altitude; the widget owns the presentation.
- tint (optional, nice-to-have): hostile → red, friendly → green, neutral → amber; flag if it costs too much on this tier (the icons already read).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-HUD] Derived HUD Tally

- definition: an optional small readout (`CONTACTS 4 · THREATS 2`), gated by `hud: true`, computed by the widget from the contact array — NOT a separate namespace. Threat count = hostiles; both counts are pure derivations of the array. Same leaf-derivation reasoning as the motion heading ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-MOTION]): the widget holds the array, so it reconstructs the tally rather than the bus transmitting it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-BODIES] The Stellar-Bodies Backdrop — `dradis/bodies`

- definition: a **second bus namespace `dradis/bodies`** (distinct from `dradis/contacts`) — a JSON **array** of stellar objects (the "terrain": star / planet / asteroid), rendered by the same `dradis_contacts` widget UNDER the contacts (a backdrop layer). Fields per body: `id` (stable string), `kind` (`star`|`planet`|`asteroid`), `type` (icon key → `bus://assets/icons/stellar/<type>.bmp`), `name`, `bearing` (degrees, same convention as contacts), `range_ls` (**light-seconds**, same physical unit as a contact's `range`), `size` (optional px), plus room for later semantic props (stellar class / planet type / mass) that ride with the body so an Intelligence or a readout can reason about the system, not just draw it.
- projection-reuse: bodies project through the **same** log light-clock as contacts ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-PROJ]) on their `range_ls` — a body sweeps inward from the edge exactly as a contact does. The projection is body/contact-agnostic; only the field name differs (`range_ls` vs `range`).
- not-tracked-targets: bodies get **no threat blink and no motion indicator** — they are the navigation reference, not tracked contacts. The alert/blink policy ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-BLINK]) and the derived motion heading ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-MOTION]) apply to `dradis/contacts` only. Bodies are also excluded from the derived HUD tally ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-HUD]) — they are not contacts and not threats.
- staged-assets: `planet_blue`, `planet_blue2`, `planet_dark`, `celestial_distant`, `asteroid_1..5`. Initial pass: **one star + one planet per system.**
- open-item-star-art: **no dedicated "star" art exists yet** — an open asset item for David; the initial star may borrow `celestial_distant`.
- constraint: the 350×350 stellar BMP32 assets are ~490 KB each — **over the 160 KB on-device image-fetch buffer.** Rendering bodies on the Qualia requires bumping the fetch/decode buffer (Qualia-only, as with the MQTT buffer) OR shipping a smaller on-device asset. A **build-time item, not a spec blocker** ([CXC-ER-RENDER]/R5 concern).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-DRADIS-IDENT] Fog-of-War Renders with ZERO Display Change — the Third Decentralization Exemplar

- definition: fog-of-war identification requires **no new display logic.** The display already maps `type → icon` and `allegiance → tint/blink`; the publisher reports `type` as the fleet's *currently sensed* classification and republishes the **same `id`** as a contact closes, so the icon **morphs in place** (`large_unknown` → the true type) and the "becomes a threat" flip is just a change to the existing `allegiance` bit. The widget grew nothing.
- key-insight: this is the **third** decentralization exemplar in this one widget — after the derived motion heading ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-MOTION]) and the derived counts/HUD ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-HUD]), which push *derivation* to the leaf, this one pushes *identification authority* to the publisher: the publishing application's sensor model (an app-layer concern) owns "what has been sensed / what is a threat", and the display consumes `type`/`allegiance` exactly as it always did. Identification is **published game state, not per-consumer re-derivation** — the display cannot recompute the true type from range (it is ground truth only the engine holds), so this is the *opposite* case from heading and correctly lives on the bus.
- contrast: unlike heading (a pure function of positions the display already holds, so bussing it would be redundant — [CXC-ER-DRADIS-MOTION]), the true type behind `large_unknown` is information the display does **not** hold; it is published, not derived.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

---

## 6. Capability: Remote Config-Reload (Bus Restart Command)

> Today an ESP32-class device fetches its `.compose.yaml` from bus_storage at **BOOT ONLY** ([DEC-FETCH-ON-BOOT-CONFIG-DISCIPLINE]), so a PUT of an updated scene does NOT take effect until the board is power-cycled — which stranded the Qualia (`(a device id, not published)`, no OTA, not USB-tethered) when an updated dradis engine-controls scene was deployed to bus_storage (2026-07-30). This capability adds the remote-reload path: an operator publishes a restart command over the bus, the device restarts, and its **existing** boot-fetch re-runs and loads the updated YAML — no power cycle, no re-flash. It lives in the shared firmware app shell (the WiFi/MQTT bus client, alongside the R1 core), so it applies to **all** ESP32-class envs (cyd, qualia_display, esp32c3, ...), not per-device.

### [CXC-ER-REMOTE-RESTART] Every ESP32-Class Device Honors a Remote-Restart Command

- definition: every ESP32-class device MUST subscribe a restart control namespace ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS]) and, on receipt of a truthy command, restart — so it re-runs the EXISTING boot-fetch path ([CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE]: WiFi → fetch `bus://assets/displays/<id>.compose.yaml` → parse → render/actuate) and loads the config that was PUT to bus_storage after its last boot. An operator pushes an updated `.compose.yaml` to bus_storage and reloads the device WITHOUT a power cycle. This is the generalized, all-devices realization of the `config/reload` follow-on proposed on [DEC-FETCH-ON-BOOT-CONFIG-DISCIPLINE].
- behavior (MVP = hard restart): on receipt → **log** the command (source namespace + payload) → **publish the pre-restart ack** ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS], `_device/<id>/status` = `"restarting"`) → **`esp_restart()`**. The reboot re-runs boot-fetch; NO new fetch/parse code is added — the capability is entirely "receive command → reboot", reusing the proven boot path. Hard restart is chosen for the MVP because it is dead-simple, reuses the existing boot-fetch, and guarantees clean state (fresh heap, fresh WiFi/bus, no partial-teardown hazard).
- scope-boundary (LOAD-BEARING — state explicitly): this is **CONFIG-reload, NOT firmware-OTA.** A remote restart re-fetches the YAML but runs the **SAME firmware** — so it fixes the common SCENE-update case (a changed composition / binding / actuator map already expressible in the running firmware). A **new firmware CAPABILITY** (e.g. a newly-ported widget renderer such as `led_bar`, a new HAL driver) is NOT delivered by this command and still requires a USB flash until a SEPARATE ESP32-OTA capability is specified (see follow-on). The spec MUST NOT be read as implying it updates firmware.
- control-plane-legitimacy: a device-lifecycle restart is a legitimate control-plane **command TO a device** — the operator requests the restart; the **device owns HOW to restart** (`esp_restart()`), and the device is the **CONSUMER of its own control namespace** (`_device/<id>/restart`). This is NOT the [CXC-PS-SEMANTIC-OWNERSHIP] anti-pattern (a *producer* emitting *consumer*-commands as its own semantic data): there is no producer publishing "what a consumer should do" here — the control namespace is addressed to the device that consumes it, exactly as `console_supervisor`'s bus-addressable lifecycle is. The `_device/` command namespace therefore does not violate the semantic-ownership rule.
- applies-to: ALL ESP32-class envs — cyd, qualia_display, esp32c3, and every future variant. The handler lives in the **shared** firmware app shell (the bus client), NOT in any per-device board profile, so bringing a new variant onto the platform inherits remote-restart for free ([CXC-ER-BOARDS] "one core, three variants").
- soft-reload (FUTURE variant, OUT OF SCOPE for the MVP): `yaml_min.h` already frees the running composition "on config reload", so a future **SOFT in-place reload** (re-fetch the YAML + rebuild the scene/bindings, WITHOUT a reboot — keeping WiFi/bus up and avoiding the reconnect cost) is a natural enhancement on the same command. It is explicitly deferred: the MVP is the hard restart. A soft reload would reuse the SAME bus contract ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS]) with a different local action.
- verify.machine: publish `_device/<id>/restart` (or the broadcast `_device/restart`) with a truthy payload → the device restarts AND re-fetches its config. Provable by ANY of: a boot-counter increment in the boot announce; the boot-fetch log line (`GET bus://assets/displays/<id>.compose.yaml`) appearing after the publish; or the observed HTTP GET of that asset at bus_storage. A host/integration test asserts the device subscribes both namespaces and calls `esp_restart()` on a truthy command and NOT on a falsy/empty one.
- verify.human: after PUTting an updated `.compose.yaml` to bus_storage and publishing the restart command, the **glass shows the updated scene** (the changed widget/binding/actuator behavior) with **no power cycle** — the operator confirms the new composition rendered/actuated, and confirms a NEW firmware capability did NOT appear (that still needs a flash), closing the config-reload-not-OTA boundary by eye.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

### [CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS] The Restart Bus Contract — `_device/<id>/restart` + `_device/restart`

- namespaces: BOTH honored. Every device subscribes **`_device/<id>/restart`** (per-device — `<id>` is the device's config id, the SAME id that keys `bus://assets/displays/<id>.compose.yaml`, e.g. `(a device id, not published)`, `(a device id, not published)`, or a `DISPLAY_DEVICE_ID` override) **AND** the broadcast **`_device/restart`** (every device honors it — a fleet-wide reload). The leading-underscore **`_device/`** prefix marks a **bus-internal control-plane** namespace (mirroring the existing `_bus/discovery/` presence announce, [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE] step 4), distinguishing it from application-semantic data — it is a command channel, not sensor/state.
- payload: a **truthy** value ⇒ restart, evaluated by the reactive_core **`dsl_truthy`** canon (the same truthiness the binary actuator HAL already uses). Accepted forms: a bare truthy scalar (`true`, `1`, `"restart"`) OR the canonical object **`{"restart": true}`**. A **falsy / empty** value (`false`, `0`, `""`, `null`, `{}`) is a **NO-OP** — so clearing the namespace does not trigger a restart. Publishers SHOULD emit the canonical `{"restart": true}`; the firmware accepts any truthy form for robustness.
- ack: RECOMMENDED, loop-closing, two-part. ⛔ THIS IS THE ACCEPTANCE EVIDENCE FOR A RESTART — a silent restart is invisible on the bus, so the device SHOULD close the loop. **Pre-restart:** publish `_device/<id>/status` = `"restarting"` (a status event) immediately before `esp_restart()`. **Post-boot:** the EXISTING presence announce (`_bus/discovery/<id>` at boot, [CXC-R-BOOT-SEQUENCE] step 4 — carrying a boot-counter is the cheapest machine signal) re-appears after reboot. The operator/monitor thus observes `restarting` → (gap) → `online/announce`, and the acceptance machine-signal ([CXC-ER-REMOTE-RESTART] verify.machine) is directly observable. `_device/<id>/status` is a device-owned status namespace (the device publishes facts about itself — semantic data it legitimately owns). ⚠ DO NOT verify a restart by UPTIME RESET: a board that publishes no `_device/<id>/heartbeat` carrying uptime makes that channel STRUCTURALLY BLIND, and a tool keyed on it reports "ACKED but no uptime reset observed" for a restart that demonstrably succeeded — measured 2026-08-16 on `hosyond_s3`. Prefer this ack pair, or the config re-fetch recorded at [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-VERIFIED].
- subscription: the device registers the two control namespaces at boot via the pattern-subscription primitive ([SPEC:CXC-PATSUB]) — two EXACT namespaces (not a broad `_device/*` glob), which on MQTT are two exact-topic subscriptions and on a future CAN transport are two frame IDs. One namespace per distinct meaning ([CXC-PS-GRANULARITY]): restart is its own frame ID, filterable at the wire.
- retained-boot-loop-guard: LOAD-BEARING boundary. The restart command MUST be published **NON-RETAINED** (edge-triggered fire-and-forget) — a *retained* truthy `_device/restart` would re-deliver to every device on every broker reconnect and restart the fleet in a loop. The firmware SHOULD additionally guard by ignoring any restart command received within a short boot window (before the first successful config-fetch completes, ~3 s), so a re-delivered, retained, or doubled command cannot loop the device. ⚠ MIGRATION HAZARD, named 2026-08-16: this clause also governs BROKER MIGRATION. Copying a broker's retained store to a new host carries any retained truthy restart with it, and a device reconnecting to the new broker receives it as a FRESH DELIVERY — which is not a boot, so the boot-window guard does not cover it. Enumerate retained `_device/` topics and decide deliberately whether the migration copies the retained store or starts clean.
- precedence: per-device vs broadcast. The two namespaces are **EQUIVALENT triggers** — both request the same action (restart), so there is no precedence CONFLICT to resolve. If BOTH arrive (a broadcast plus a targeted command), the device restarts **once**: the first truthy command wins and the device reboots; any second command lands in the boot-guard window and is dropped. Neither namespace overrides the other; the boot-guard makes the double-fire idempotent.
- robustness: a malformed / non-truthy payload is a NO-OP (never a crash, never an unintended reboot); a command for a different `<id>` is not delivered (exact-namespace subscription); a restart mid-render is safe (hard restart discards all in-flight state by construction).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]

